==========================================================================================================
HOW TO READ THIS
==========================================================================================================
  FROZEN pairs carry NO information about mean reversion. Their alpha is 1
  because neither price moved, which is arithmetic, not economics. If they
  are the majority, the pooled alpha is mostly measuring how often Kalshi
  ladder legs go untraded at a new price -- not whether the gap reverts.

  So read the MOVED block as the real test:

    alpha well below 1 in MOVED  ->  the gap DOES revert once the market
      reprices, and 'the ladder gap is a random walk' is wrong as stated.
      The correct claim becomes: violations persist because the legs do not
      reprice, which is a LIMITS-TO-ARBITRAGE result, not a no-signal one.
      It also means the training metric was diluted, and the ladder loss
      should be restricted to pairs where a leg actually moved.

    alpha near 1 in MOVED too    ->  the original conclusion survives a test
      it had not previously faced, and can be stated more strongly.

  Either way, compare P(viol) in MOVED against ALL. diagnose_staleness.py
  already reported survival collapsing to 33.5% at h=1 once a leg reprices;
  this should reproduce something close to that, and if it does not, the two
  measurements disagree and that has to be resolved before either is used.
